The network is fully healthy. Exhaustive testing shows that host_1_1_1_1
(10.1.1.2/24, gateway 10.1.1.1) can successfully reach every server in the
10.200.0.0/24 range: all four web servers (10.200.0.3–.6) and the DNS server
(10.200.0.2) responded to pings with 0% packet loss. The reverse path
(web_server_0 → host_1_1_1_1) is equally healthy, confirming full
bidirectional reachability.

Every layer of the forwarding path was verified. The L2 access path
(host_1_1_1_1 → switch_access_1_1_1 → switch_dist_1_1) is operational:
the host's MAC is correctly learned on switch_access_1_1_1 eth1 and the
bridge on switch_dist_1_1 correctly presents IP 10.1.1.1 as the default
gateway. The OSPF routing domain (area 1 stub anchored by switch_dist_1_1
and switch_dist_2_1, area 0 backbone across router_core_1/2/3 and
switch_server_access) is fully converged: all neighbors are in Full state,
and the inter-area route 10.200.0.0/24 is correctly distributed from area
0 into area 1 via router_core_1 acting as ABR. No ACL, route-map, or
traffic policy exists anywhere in the path that could filter traffic.

The user's belief that their gateway or uplink is misconfigured is
incorrect — both the IP address (10.1.1.2/24) and the default route
(via 10.1.1.1) are properly set and the gateway responds immediately.
The reported outage does not exist in the current lab state; this ticket
represents a false premise. No corrective action is needed. If the user
observed a transient outage, it was likely OSPF convergence delay in the
moments immediately after the lab was started, which has since resolved
naturally.
